# Hostile audit — two-half-disk remainder/sign localization

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Audited source:** research/target_c_halfdisk_remainder_sign_173_2026-09-12.md  
**Disposition:** PASS at conditional proof-only scope; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.

The audit checks that the two-half-disk group areas can be inserted into the
finite Section-160 component inequality without a hidden local/global
identification, and then checks the sign algebra in Section 172.

## 1. Internal rhombus area

The groupwise atomic theorem supplies, on one common finite diagonal,

\[
\sum_{r\in\mathcal G_i}A_{4,r}
=\left(\frac{\pi}{8}-o(1)\right)D^2.
\]

Section 160 uses only rhombi whose four vertices are unloaded. A loaded vertex
has at most five unit neighbors in a triangle-free source, since consecutive
neighbor rays are separated by more than \(\pi/3\). Hence it is incident to
only \(O(1)\) strict rhombus faces. The loaded force-hull population is \(O(D)\).
The local-rhombus incidence audit bounds the number of times one physical
local rhombus can be charged. Since each unit rhombus has area at most one,
the total loaded-touching correction is \(O(D)\), giving

\[
\sum_{r\in\mathcal G_i}A_{4,r}^{\mathrm{int}}
=\left(\frac{\pi}{8}-o(1)\right)D^2.
\]

No claim is made that every local rhombus is a global face, and no isolated
source point is removed.

## 2. Summation of Section 160

For each component, Section 160 gives

\[
r_r+\eta_r\ge
\sqrt{\pi A_{4,r}^{\mathrm{int}}}+2-d_r.
\]

Rearranging and summing over a group gives

\[
\sum d_r+\sum(r_r+\eta_r)
\ge \sum\sqrt{\pi A_{4,r}^{\mathrm{int}}}+2N_i.
\]

The \(2N_i\) term is nonnegative. Cauchy in the form
\(\sum\sqrt{a_j}\ge\sqrt{\sum a_j}\) gives the lower bound
\(\pi D/(2\sqrt2)-o(D)\) for each group. The two group index sets are
disjoint, so their component terms are disjoint subterms of

\[
S_D+G_D
=\sum_r d_r+R_I+s_B+\sum_r\eta_r.
\]

Dropping all remaining nonnegative terms is therefore legitimate and yields
\((S_D+G_D)/D\ge\pi/\sqrt2-o(1)\).

## 3. Sign identity

From \(G_D=2+q+m-k-S_D\),

\[
q+m=S_D+G_D+k-2.
\]

Subtracting \(\operatorname{per}(F)\) gives

\[
\chi_D=S_D+G_D+k-2-\operatorname{per}(F).
\]

Type-II perimeter convergence is \(\operatorname{per}(F)/D\to\pi\).
Consequently the base lower bound is

\[
\frac{\pi}{\sqrt2}-\pi
=-\pi\left(1-\frac1{\sqrt2}\right)
=-0.920151184510610\ldots.
\]

The finite outer-walk identity is
\[
b-\operatorname{per}(F)=u_B+\chi_D\ge0.
\]
It supports only the stated conditional dichotomy: a negative \(\chi_D\) must
be paid by \(u_B\). The audit rejects any inference that the lower bound on
\(\chi_D\) makes \(u_B\) uniformly positive or makes \(\chi_D\) nonnegative.

## 4. Scope

The result is conditional on the proof-only two-half-disk alternative and the
audited Section-160/172 inputs. It does not address the center-wrapping branch,
the Type-I inactive-container branch, or the source-preserving minimality
bridge. It is a valid proof-only candidate, not a C-II closure.
